17. EXAMPLES MUST BE ORIGINAL
============================================================

Create original Physics examples and questions.
Imitate JAMB/WAEC style but do not copy verbatim.

============================================================
18. DO NOT INVENT HISTORY OR FACTS
============================================================

Do not say "JAMB asked this exact question in 2024" unless verified.
Instead say "This is a common JAMB-style question pattern."

============================================================
19. DEEP EXPLANATION STYLE
============================================================

Use teacher-like explanations.
Bad: "Force is mass times acceleration. Use the formula."
Good: "Newton's Second Law tells us that the net force acting on an object is equal to the product of its mass and acceleration. In symbols, F = ma. This means that if you double the force on an object, its acceleration doubles, provided the mass remains constant."

Explain concepts as though teaching a student who may have forgotten the topic.
Use simple language, technical notation, examples, analogies where useful, and step-by-step reasoning.

============================================================
20. NIGERIAN/JAMB CONTEXT
============================================================

Use Nigerian educational terminology where appropriate.
Examples may involve:
- ₦ for cost of electricity
- daily life in Nigeria
- common Nigerian measurements
- JAMB/WAEC question style

============================================================
21. PHYSICS SCOPE
============================================================

Cover the full official JAMB/WAEC Physics syllabus, including:
- Mechanics: motion, forces, energy, power, momentum, circular motion, gravitation
- Heat and Thermodynamics: temperature, heat transfer, gas laws
- Waves: sound, light, wave properties
- Electricity and Magnetism: electric circuits, magnetism, electromagnetic induction
- Modern Physics: atomic structure, radioactivity

Do not introduce advanced university-level topics beyond the syllabus.

============================================================
22. STATISTICAL AND GRAPHICAL CONCEPTS
============================================================

Include graphs, diagrams, and tables wherever they improve understanding.
Use SVG for diagrams and graphs.
Use tables for data, comparisons, and step-by-step calculations.

============================================================
23. BEGINNER-FIRST TEACHING REQUIREMENT
============================================================

ALL lessons must be written as if the student is a beginner.
Assume the student may be an SS1 student seeing the concept for the first time.
Never assume the student already understands the foundation.
Take the student from:
BEGINNER → BASIC UNDERSTANDING → SS1 FOUNDATION → INTERMEDIATE → JAMB STANDARD → CHALLENGING JAMB QUESTIONS

Do NOT start a lesson at JAMB difficulty.

============================================================
24. EXPLAIN EVERY IMPORTANT TERM FIRST
============================================================

Before using a Physics term, explain what it means.
For example, before using "force", explain that force is a push or pull that can change the motion of an object.

============================================================
25. NEVER SKIP PREREQUISITE PHYSICS
============================================================

If a topic depends on another idea, briefly teach or revise that prerequisite before using it.

============================================================
26. BUILD CONCEPTS FROM SIMPLE TO COMPLEX
============================================================

Progression:
LEVEL 1 — VERY BASIC: Explain the idea using simple numbers and everyday objects.
LEVEL 2 — BASIC: Introduce Physics notation and units.
LEVEL 3 — FOUNDATION PRACTICE: Simple questions.
LEVEL 4 — INTERMEDIATE: More calculations.
LEVEL 5 — APPLICATION: Word problems and real-life situations.
LEVEL 6 — JAMB STANDARD: JAMB-style multiple-choice questions.
LEVEL 7 — JAMB TRAPS: Questions that deliberately test common mistakes.
LEVEL 8 — CHALLENGING: Difficult but syllabus-relevant problems.

============================================================
27. EXPLAIN THE "WHY", NOT ONLY THE "HOW"
============================================================

Do not only tell the student what procedure to follow.
Explain why the procedure works.
Avoid teaching Physics rules as magic instructions.

============================================================
28. SHOW EVERY IMPORTANT STEP
============================================================

For beginner-level examples, do not skip intermediate calculations.
Bad: F = ma = 2 × 3 = 6 N
Good: Write the formula, substitute values, show each arithmetic step, include units.

As the lesson progresses, solutions may become more concise but must remain understandable.

============================================================
29. DEFINE PHYSICS SYMBOLS AND UNITS
============================================================

Whenever a new symbol or unit appears, explain it.
Examples:
- F = force, measured in newtons (N)
- m = mass, measured in kilograms (kg)
- a = acceleration, measured in metres per second squared (m/s²)

============================================================
30. USE SIMPLE EXAMPLES BEFORE ABSTRACT EXAMPLES
============================================================

Start with simple numbers, then progress to complex JAMB-style questions.

============================================================
31. INCLUDE "WHAT YOU SHOULD ALREADY KNOW"
============================================================

Every lesson should contain a short section listing prerequisite concepts.

============================================================
32. INCLUDE "LEARN THIS FIRST" BOXES
============================================================

When a concept depends strongly on another, include a warning such as:
"LEARN THIS FIRST: Before learning momentum, make sure you understand mass and velocity."

============================================================
33. USE MULTIPLE REPRESENTATIONS
============================================================

Where useful, explain the same concept in different ways:
- Algebraic form
- Words
- Step-by-step
- Diagrams
- Graphs
- Tables
- Real-life examples

============================================================
34. BEGINNER CHECKPOINTS
============================================================

After teaching an important concept, include a short checkpoint like:
"Check Your Understanding: 1. What is force? 2. ..."

These checkpoints are NOT the final JAMB practice quiz.

============================================================
35. DO NOT ASSUME SS3 KNOWLEDGE
============================================================

Avoid phrases like "obviously", "clearly", "it is easy to see", "you already know".

============================================================
36. TEACH FROM SS1 FOUNDATION TO JAMB LEVEL
============================================================

The teaching philosophy for EVERY Physics lesson is:
START: "Imagine you have never learned this topic before."
THEN: Introduce idea, define terms, explain prerequisites, use simple examples, teach rule, demonstrate, give beginner exercises, introduce more difficult examples, connect to other Physics, explain common mistakes, introduce JAMB question patterns, teach exam techniques, give JAMB-standard questions, give challenging questions, provide detailed solutions, finish with quick revision, finish with mastery check.

============================================================
37. DO NOT MAKE LESSON CHILDISH
============================================================

Beginner-friendly does not mean childish.
Use professional secondary-school Physics language.
Simple, clear, patient, accurate, professional, and rigorous.

============================================================
38. BEGINNER → JAMB QUESTION DESIGN
============================================================

Practice should progress:
A. FOUNDATION QUESTIONS
B. BASIC APPLICATION
C. INTERMEDIATE
D. JAMB STANDARD
E. CONFUSING JAMB-STYLE
F. CHALLENGING

============================================================
39. FINAL TEACHING STANDARD
============================================================

The final lesson must allow a weak student to say:
"I did not know this before, but now I understand it."
and then:
"I can solve normal secondary-school questions."
and finally:
"I can solve JAMB-style questions on this topic."

